course: dictionary
lesson: 3076
title: A grand word for a grand building
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An edifice is a large, grand or impressive building, like a cathedral, a palace or a tall tower. It is a formal word, so you will mostly find it in older books, guidebooks and serious writing. Context clues help: if the sentence describes a huge stone building with towers, then edifice means that building.
explain: Edifice has a second meaning too. Used figuratively, it is a big system or set of ideas built up over time, which could fall apart. Clues like "built up over many years" or "could collapse" tell you this is the meaning.
explain: Remember that the goal is the right word, not the longest one. «He ambulated to the edifice» sounds like a robot swallowed a dictionary. «He walked to the building» says the same thing clearly, and that is usually better.
fact: Edifice is a formal noun: an edifice, several edifices.
fact: «Edifice» means a large, grand or impressive building.
fact: Phrases you may see are a grand edifice, a stone edifice and the whole edifice.
fact: Figuratively, «edifice» means a big system or set of ideas built up over time, which could fall apart.
fact: Choose the word that fits clearly; «He walked to the building» beats «He ambulated to the edifice».
exercise: «The old cathedral was a grand stone edifice with tall towers.» What is an edifice here? | A type of tall tree | A loud noise in the street | *A large, grand building | A small wooden shed
exercise: «The whole edifice of his beliefs was built up over many years.» What does edifice mean here? | *A big set of ideas built up over time | A tall stone tower | A small, quick shop | A loud argument at school
exercise: Which sentence uses the clearer word for a story? | He ambulated to the edifice | *He walked to the building | He perambulated towards the edifice
test: What does «edifice» mean? | A type of bird | *A large, grand or impressive building | A kind of soup | A small, quiet garden
test: Which phrase uses edifice correctly? | a sticky edifice | a sleepy edifice | a hungry edifice | *a tall stone edifice
exam: Mia says «edifice» is just a fancy word for house, so she should always use it. Why is that a poor choice? | *A simpler word like building is clearer, and the right word matters more than the longest one | Edifice means a house, so it is always the best word | Edifice means a river | Long words always make writing better
